ios: touch wording (tap, long-press and drag in the game's instructions)

Tutorial Island tells new players to "use your left mouse-button to click", that "Right clicking will also give more
options" and to "use your keyboard's arrow keys to rotate the view"; dialogues and message boxes say "right click",
"left click" and "mouse over". On a touch screen every text the server sets (IF_SETTEXT) goes through the touch wording
table of the new module TouchWording.ts (overrides), and so do the interfaces' own texts once they are loaded ("Click
here to continue" -> "Tap here to continue", "Click here to logout", the shop's "Right-click on shop to buy item"), as
well as the tutorial box's "Click to continue". Without a touch screen nothing changes.

The table holds exact, case-sensitive phrases taken from the rev-274 content, applied one line at a time: "use your left
mouse-button to click" -> "tap", "left-mouse button" -> "finger", "left click(ing on)" -> "tap(ping)", "Right clicking"
-> "Long-pressing", "right click (on)" / "right-click (on)" -> "long-press", "click(ing) on" -> "tap(ping)", "use your
keyboard's arrow keys to rotate the view" -> "drag the view with your finger to turn it", "by pressing the arrow keys"
-> "by dragging it", "move your mouse over" / "put the mouse over" -> "touch", "with your mouse" -> "with your
finger", "found at the bottom right" -> "found at the right" (the mobile layout's tabs are a column on the right). The
server splits long texts into lines itself (split_init with the font's metrics) and sends each line on its own, so a
phrase broken over two lines has an entry for each piece, anchored to the start or end of a line. Checked against every
script text that mentions mouse, click, arrow keys or keyboard (96 lines rewritten): each rewritten line stays within
the width the server split it to (450 px of q8 in the tutorial box, 380 in dialogues, 470 in message boxes), and the
static texts within their components. A mouse that is an animal and a click that is a sound are left alone.

